// compiler/src/build_cache.sfn
//
// Content-addressed build artifact cache for the unified build
// driver — Stage C of `docs/proposals/0006-build-architecture.md`.
//
// This module is the foundation for incremental builds. PR1 ships
// the pure cache surface (key derivation, layout, lookup, store)
// with unit-test coverage; the wiring into `_compile_capsule_path`
// and `sfn build -p` follows in PR1b.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Cache key contract (proposal §4.12)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   key = sha256(
//       sha256(source_bytes)
//       || sha256(sorted(dep_slug || sha256(dep.layout-manifest_bytes), …))
//       || compiler_version
//       || canonical(flags)
//       || slug
//       || "\ntgt3:" || target_triple
//   )
//
// The target component is SFN-677, promoted from `target_os` to the
// TRIPLE by SFN-774 / SFEP-0068 §3.6 (the `tgt1:` -> `tgt2:` marker bump
// below). The emitted `.ll` is genuinely target-dependent —
// `llvm/lowering_debug_state.sfn` reads the resolved target at emit time
// to select the errno locator, the sysconf values and the Windows legs —
// so without it, one source at one compiler version produced different
// IR under a single key, and a warm shared cache could hand a build IR
// emitted for another OS. `canonical(flags)` cannot stand in: both driver
// call sites pass the empty canonical form (`capsule_resolver/compile.sfn`).
// The OS alone was already too coarse once a single OS could carry two
// ABIs (`x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` vs `x86_64-w64-mingw32`): both folded to
// `"Windows"` and would have shared a cache entry despite compiling to
// different objects — exactly the SFN-677 defect, one axis wider.
//
// Each input separator is `\n` so that two distinct tuples cannot
// collide via concatenation (e.g. `("ab","c")` vs `("a","bc")`).
// Within the dep component a `\n` separates each dep's slug from its
// manifest hash and `|` joins the sorted pairs, so the guarantee is
// unconditional only for slugs free of those two bytes. Every slug
// the resolver produces is a `/`-joined path stem, so this holds in
// practice; it is not enforced.
//
// `target_triple` is the one component with NO such structural guarantee —
// it may originate from an unvalidated `SAILFIN_TARGET_TRIPLE`/
// `SAILFIN_TARGET_OS` env string that can contain any byte. It is
// therefore placed last, with nothing following it: the first
// `\ntgt3:` splits the tail unambiguously (no slug can contain that
// sequence), so whatever `target_triple` holds, it cannot absorb a
// following component and alias another tuple. Appending anything
// after it would forfeit that. See `cache_key_for`.
//
// No schema bump accompanied SFN-677, matching the SFN-661 precedent
// below: a key only ever gained a component, so no pre-SFN-677 entry
// is reachable by a post-SFN-677 key — stale entries are orphaned,
// never mismatched. The first build after it lands re-emits every
// module once. That is expected, not a regression. SFN-774's `tgt1:` ->
// `tgt2:` marker bump follows the same no-collision logic one level
// deeper: it is not a schema version bump, just a guarantee that an
// OS-keyed entry from before the triple promotion can never be read as
// (and silently misinterpreted as) a triple-keyed one.
//
// Sorting the dep components makes the key invariant under dep
// enumeration order — important for parallel builds and for
// resolver implementations that walk graphs in different orders.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Why the dep component carries the dep's SLUG, not just its bytes
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A module's emitted symbols are a function of two things: its own
// source, and the *resolved provider slug* of each of its imports.
// Lowering mangles every cross-module call site as
// `<symbol>__<sanitize_module_suffix(provider_slug)>`
// (`llvm/lowering/lowering_helpers_mangling.sfn`), and the provider
// slug is resolved from the import path against the staged artifacts
// — `./declarations` resolves to `parser/declarations` when
// `parser/declarations.layout-manifest` exists, and falls back to
// `parser/declarations/mod` when it does not
// (`llvm/imports.sfn:resolve_import_artifact_slug`).
//
// Hashing only the dep manifest's *content* therefore under-determines
// the key: two different provider modules whose layout manifests
// happen to be byte-identical are indistinguishable to a dependent.
// That is not a corner case — a module declaring no structs emits an
// EMPTY `.layout-manifest`, so every layout-free provider collides
// with every other one.
//
// SFN-661 is exactly that collision. Splitting
// `parser/declarations.sfn` into `parser/declarations/{mod,functions,…}.sfn`
// moved the provider slug for an unchanged `parser/statements.sfn`
// from `parser/declarations` to `parser/declarations/mod`, re-mangling
// its call sites from `parse_function__parser__declarations` to
// `parse_function__parser__declarations__mod`. Both manifests were
// empty, `statements.sfn`'s own bytes were unchanged, and the version
// / flags / slug inputs were unchanged — so the key was identical, the
// pre-split `.ll` was served, and the link failed on undefined
// references to symbols that no longer existed.
//
// Mixing the dep slug in makes the key capture the mangling contract
// exactly: a dependent's key changes if and only if an input to its
// emitted symbols changed. It is not an over-approximation — a barrel
// module that keeps its slug while re-pointing a re-export still emits
// the same forwarding shim under the same name, so its dependents
// legitimately keep hitting.
//
// `canonical(flags)` is `canonicalize_flags(flags)` — a sorted JSON
// array of strings.
//
// `slug` (schema v2) is the per-enumeration module slug. The cache
// is content-addressed by source bytes + dep manifests + version +
// flags, but the *same* source can be enumerated under two distinct
// slugs (e.g. `/foo` via the host-capsule walker and `../src/foo`
// via the relative-import walker). Without slug in the key those two
// compiles produce identical keys and race on the same cache entry.
// Mixing slug in keeps them distinct. See #527.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// On-disk layout
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   <root>/<key[0..2]>/<key>/{ir.sfn-asm, layout.manifest, mod.ll, mod.o}
//
// `<root>` defaults to a shared per-user directory
// (`$XDG_CACHE_HOME/sailfin` or `$HOME/.cache/sailfin`) so identical
// module compiles are deduplicated across projects on a machine
// (SFEP-0040 §3.1). It can be overridden by `$SAILFIN_BUILD_CACHE_DIR`
// (shared CI caches), falls back to in-tree `build/cache` when `$HOME`
// is unresolvable, and is pinned in-tree for the compiler self-host
// build so `make compile` / `make check` stay hermetic. See
// `cache_root_from`. The trailing suffix is
// `build_cache_schema_version()` (currently `v2`) — bump it when a
// key-shape or layout change could make an old entry ALIAS a new one,
// and let the old `v<N>` tree age out so stale entries can never
// poison new compilers.
//
// A change that only makes keys *more* distinguishing does not need a
// bump: every old entry simply becomes unaddressable and ages out on
// its own. SFN-661 (folding the dep slug into the dep component) is
// that case — bumping would have discarded every warm CI cache to no
// benefit, since no v2 entry can be reached by a post-SFN-661 key.
//
// The 2-character prefix shard caps the directory fan-out at 256
// children, preventing the one-directory-with-millions-of-entries
// problem that bites https://stackoverflow.com/q/466521 large caches.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Test surface
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// All `![io]` operations are parameterized on `root` so tests can
// pass an arbitrary tmp directory without setting environment
// variables. The convenience reader `cache_root(capsule_name)` is the
// production-side wrapper that resolves the root ladder from the
// environment; the pure `cache_root_from` underneath it takes every
// input explicitly so the precedence order is unit-testable.
import {
    _atomic_rename_into_place,
    _copy_file,
    _dirname_cmd,
    _ensure_dir_recursive_cmd,
    _mktemp_sibling_cmd,
    _sanitize_filename_cmd,
    _sha256_of_file,
    _shell_read_cmd,
    _shell_single_quote_arg,
    _write_text_cmd
} from "./build/fs";
import {
    list_child_dirs,
    remove_empty_dirs_under,
    remove_tree,
    walk_dirs
} from "./build/fs_tree";
import { sha256_hex_of_string } from "./build/hash";
import { host_is_windows } from "./build/target";
import { _get_env_cmd } from "./build_flags";
import { char_code_at_text, find_last_index_of_char, substring } from "./string_utils";

// The Windows-only native probes backing the cache GC helpers below
// (SFN-754). Declared module-locally per the SFN-720 convention
// `build/fs.sfn` documents for `sfn_rename_replace`/`sfn_create_exclusive`
// — an extern over a runtime symbol resolves against the pinned seed and
// needs no seed cut, unlike importing the defining module.
//
// `sfn_fs_stat_size`/`sfn_fs_stat_mtime_epoch` are defined by
// `runtime/sfn/platform/file_stat.sfn` (POSIX stub, always `-1`) and
// swapped for `file_stat_windows.sfn` (real `GetFileAttributesExA` read)
// by the matching Windows table in `runtime/capsule.toml`. Both
// return `-1` to mean "not available, fall back to the shell reading" —
// the POSIX leg always does, since `struct stat` field offsets are not
// portable across glibc/Darwin (`runtime/sfn/platform/sizes_linux.sfn`).
extern fn sfn_fs_stat_size(path: *u8) -> i64;

extern fn sfn_fs_stat_mtime_epoch(path: *u8) -> i64;

// The write-side counterpart of the two probes above, backing
// `cache_touch_entry` below. Same `-1`-means-"not available" contract:
// the POSIX stub always returns it, so `cache_touch_entry` unconditionally
// falls back to the pre-existing `process.run(["touch", dir])` there.
extern fn sfn_fs_touch_now(path: *u8) -> i32;

// The existing runtime realtime clock (`runtime/sfn/clock.sfn`), real on
// every target — POSIX and Windows both resolve the `clock_gettime` it
// calls (the Windows leg through `sfn/platform/clock_windows.sfn`,
// added by the matching manifest target table). Out-param + status
// return, matching the defining signature exactly rather than the
// simpler plain-return shape a fresh symbol would get.
extern fn sfn_clock_unix_millis(out_millis: *i64) -> i32;

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Schema version
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Bumped when the cache key derivation, on-disk layout, or
// artifact schema changes in a way that makes old entries
// incompatible with the current compiler. Old `v<N>/` trees are
// left in place; they age out via the user's normal cache-cleanup
// or `--clean` flow once that ships in PR1b.
fn build_cache_schema_version() -> string { return "v2"; }

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Public types
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
struct CacheKey {
    digest: string;
}

struct CacheEntryPaths {
    sfn_asm: string;
    layout_manifest: string;
    ll: string;
    obj: string;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Cache stats
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Per-build counters surfaced to the CLI's end-of-build summary
// (and, in a future PR, to `sfn build --json`'s build report).
// All fields are simple counters with no allocation, so an empty
// build that never touches the cache produces a single
// zero-valued struct.
//
// Field semantics:
//   - `hits` — the cache served the artifact; the producer pass
//     was skipped entirely.
//   - `misses` — no cached artifact for the key; the producer ran.
//   - `stores` — a fresh artifact was successfully written into
//     the cache after a miss. Counted independently of `misses`
//     because a `cp` failure (e.g. read-only cache root) flips a
//     successful build into "miss-but-not-stored", which CI may
//     want to alarm on.
//   - `invalid_keys` — `_is_valid_digest` rejected a digest before
//     any filesystem access. Non-zero in production indicates a
//     hash failure (source unreadable or allocation failure) — the
//     build still succeeds, but cache hit rate goes to zero. CI
//     gates can alarm on `invalid_keys > 0`.
//   - `copy_failures` — the cache contained the artifact but
//     `cache_copy_artifact_to` failed to materialise it (e.g.
//     destination filesystem full). Treated as a miss for the
//     producer, but tracked separately so the operator can
//     distinguish "cache was empty" from "cache was poisoned".
struct CacheStats {
    hits: int;
    misses: int;
    stores: int;
    invalid_keys: int;
    copy_failures: int;
}

fn empty_cache_stats() -> CacheStats {
    return CacheStats {
        hits: 0,
        misses: 0,
        stores: 0,
        invalid_keys: 0,
        copy_failures: 0
    };
}

// Field-wise sum of two `CacheStats`. Pure. Used to fold a
// separately-accumulated layer's counters into the build-wide
// total before the CLI prints the `[cache]` summary — e.g. the
// runtime C/LL object cache (`cli_main.sfn`) accumulates its own
// hits/misses while compiling `runtime/native/src/*` objects, then
// merges them into the `.ll` module cache's `CacheStats` so the
// single summary line reflects both layers (#915).
fn merge_cache_stats(a: CacheStats, b: CacheStats) -> CacheStats {
    return CacheStats {
        hits: a.hits + b.hits,
        misses: a.misses + b.misses,
        stores: a.stores + b.stores,
        invalid_keys: a.invalid_keys + b.invalid_keys,
        copy_failures: a.copy_failures + b.copy_failures
    };
}

// True iff the build produced no cache activity at all (zero
// hits and zero misses). Useful for deciding whether to print
// the cache summary line — empty stats from a manifest-less
// project shouldn't show a `[cache] hits=0 misses=0 ...` line.
fn cache_stats_is_empty(stats: CacheStats) -> boolean {
    if stats.hits != 0 { return false; }
    if stats.misses != 0 { return false; }
    if stats.stores != 0 { return false; }
    if stats.invalid_keys != 0 { return false; }
    if stats.copy_failures != 0 { return false; }
    return true;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Build-cache config (CLI flags + env-var overrides)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Resolved once at the top of a build and threaded through the
// resolver. Lets `sfn build --no-cache` and `sfn build --clean`
// override the env-var defaults without setenv (Sailfin's runtime
// env-var contract is read-only).
//
// Resolution rules:
//   - `enabled` is true unless `SAILFIN_NO_CACHE=1` OR `--no-cache`.
//   - `trace` is true if `SAILFIN_CACHE_TRACE=1` OR `--cache-trace`.
//   - `clean` flips on only via `--clean`; no env-var equivalent
//     because wiping the cache is a destructive op the user must
//     opt into explicitly per invocation.
struct BuildCacheConfig {
    enabled: boolean;
    trace: boolean;
    clean: boolean;
    // The current build's `[capsule].name`, threaded here so it
    // reaches every `cache_root()` call site (this config already
    // flows to all of them). Used only to pin the content-addressed
    // cache root in-tree for the compiler self-host build
    // (`== "sfn/compiler"`, SFEP-0040 §3.1) so `make compile` / `make
    // check` never read a developer's global `~/.cache/sailfin`.
    // Empty for positional/foreign builds → global default root.
    capsule_name: string;
}

fn empty_build_cache_config() -> BuildCacheConfig {
    return BuildCacheConfig {
        enabled: true,
        trace: false,
        clean: false,
        capsule_name: ""
    };
}

// `no_cache_flag` / `trace_flag` / `clean_flag` are the CLI
// switches; this helper folds them with the env-var defaults.
fn resolve_build_cache_config(no_cache_flag: boolean, trace_flag: boolean, clean_flag: boolean) -> BuildCacheConfig ![io] {
    let env_disabled = strings_equal(_get_env_cmd("SAILFIN_NO_CACHE"), "1");
    let env_trace = strings_equal(_get_env_cmd("SAILFIN_CACHE_TRACE"), "1");
    return BuildCacheConfig {
        enabled: !env_disabled && !no_cache_flag,
        trace: env_trace || trace_flag,
        clean: clean_flag,
        capsule_name: ""
    };
}

// Wipe the schema-versioned subtree under `root`. Returns true on
// success (or no-op when the directory didn't exist), false if the tree
// could not be fully removed. `remove_tree` carries the empty-path and
// `"/"` refusals.
//
// Note: deletes the FULL `<root>/v<N>/...` tree the cache stores
// under. Callers should resolve `cache_root(capsule_name)` first and
// pass that — wiping the bare cache base (e.g. `$SAILFIN_BUILD_CACHE_DIR`)
// would nuke any sibling schema versions that future compilers might
// be relying on.
fn cache_clean(root: string) -> boolean ![io] { return remove_tree(root); }

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Cache GC: mtime-on-hit, info, prune, stale-schema sweep
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// SFEP-0040 §3.2–3.4. All operate on a resolved schema root
// (`<base>/v<N>`) and never touch the cache *key*, entry *layout*, or
// the atomic-publish protocol — only when entries are removed and the
// recency signal `cache_prune` reads. Every removal path is `![io]`, the
// same effect class the cache already carries. Enumeration and removal
// are shell-free; the remaining shell-quoted reads below (`stat`, `du`,
// `date`) are what SFN-754 replaces.
// A cache HIT copies artifacts OUT of the entry directory into the
// build tree; it never writes into the entry itself, so the entry's
// mtime would otherwise stay pinned at creation time and `cache_prune`
// would evict a hot entry as though it were cold. Touch the entry
// directory on every hit so mtime is a true LRU recency signal
// (SFEP-0040 §3.4). Metadata-only; a failure is non-fatal (best-effort
// recency — the build has already succeeded).
fn cache_touch_entry(root: string, key: CacheKey) -> boolean ![io] {
    if !_is_valid_digest(key.digest) { return false; }
    let dir = _cache_entry_dir(root, key);
    if !fs.exists(dir) { return false; }
    // The age axis is live on Windows now (`_now_epoch`/`_entry_mtime_epoch`
    // both have a real native path there, SFN-754), so a hit here has to
    // genuinely refresh recency or `cache_prune --max-age` sweeps entries
    // that were just served. `sfn_fs_touch_now` (native `SetFileTime`) is
    // what keeps that true on Windows; POSIX still shells `touch`
    // unconditionally, since the native probe there is an always-`-1`
    // stub, matching `_entry_mtime_epoch`/`_dir_size_bytes` above.
    if sfn_fs_touch_now(dir as *u8) == 0 { return true; }
    return process.run(["touch", dir]) == 0;
}

// Parse a leading run of decimal digits as a non-negative int. Returns
// 0 when `text` has no leading digit (shell helpers below already trim
// their output, so a well-formed number is the whole string).
fn _parse_nonneg_int(text: string) -> int {
    let mut val: int = 0;
    let mut i: int = 0;
    loop {
        if i >= text.length { break; }
        let code = char_code_at_text(text, i);
        if code < 48 || code > 57 { break; }
        val = val * 10 + (code - 48);
        i += 1;
    }
    return val;
}

fn _basename_cmd(path: string) -> string {
    let idx = find_last_index_of_char(path, "/");
    if idx < 0 { return path; }
    return substring(path, idx + 1, path.length);
}

// The LIVE `<prefix>/<key>` entry directories under `root` (layout depth
// 2) — those still holding at least one artifact.
//
// The emptiness filter is deliberate and is this helper's own policy:
// `cache_info` should not count an entry holding nothing, and
// `cache_prune` should not carry one through the LRU machinery to
// reclaim what `_prune_empty_shards` reaps at the end of the same prune
// anyway. Note the directory is not weightless — `_dir_size_bytes` is
// `du -sk`, so an empty one measures a block (~4 KiB), the same cruft
// `cache_sweep_stale_schemas` reclaims below. The reap is the reason to
// filter here, not a zero size.
//
// Until SFN-756 the filter was implicit: `walk_dirs` classified a child
// as a directory by whether listing it yielded entries, so an
// artifact-less entry directory was invisible to the walk itself. That
// walk now uses `fs.is_directory` and answers correctly, so the policy
// has to be stated here or it silently inverts. The filter reuses the
// same `fs.listDirectory` the old heuristic spent — and inherits its
// failure mode, since an unreadable directory collapses to zero entries
// and is filtered out either way — but per entry the walk now pays a
// `stat` on top of that `opendir` rather than the `opendir` alone.
//
// The policy does NOT extend to `cache_sweep_stale_schemas`, where the
// same invisibility was a latent bug rather than an intent; see the note
// there.
fn _cache_entry_dirs(root: string) -> string[] ![io] {
    let dirs = walk_dirs(root, 2, 2);
    let mut live: string[] = [];
    let mut i: int = 0;
    loop {
        if i >= dirs.length { break; }
        if fs.listDirectory(dirs[i]).length > 0 { live.push(dirs[i]); }
        i += 1;
    }
    return live;
}

// Directory mtime in epoch seconds. Tries the native Windows probe first
// (`sfn_fs_stat_mtime_epoch`, real only on a Windows target — see the
// extern's header note); its `-1` "not available" sentinel is always what
// the POSIX stub returns, so POSIX unconditionally falls through to the
// pre-existing shell reading below, byte-identical to before SFN-754.
// That fallback tries GNU `stat -c %Y` then BSD `stat -f %m` so the LRU
// signal is portable across Linux and macOS.
fn _entry_mtime_epoch(dir: string) -> int ![io] {
    let native = sfn_fs_stat_mtime_epoch(dir as *u8);
    if native >= 0 { return native as int; }
    let q = _shell_single_quote_arg(dir);
    let out = _shell_read_cmd("stat -c %Y " + q + " 2>/dev/null || stat -f %m "
        + q
        + " 2>/dev/null");
    return _parse_nonneg_int(out);
}

// On-disk size of `dir` in bytes. Tries the native Windows per-file walk
// first (`_dir_size_bytes_native`); its `-1` sentinel is always what a
// POSIX host produces (the underlying probe is the same always-`-1` stub
// `_entry_mtime_epoch` falls back from), so POSIX unconditionally falls
// through to the pre-existing shell reading below, byte-identical to
// before SFN-754. `du -sk` (1024-byte blocks) is portable across GNU and
// BSD; block-rounding is the correct notion of "on-disk size" and is
// precise enough for both `info` and LRU compares.
fn _dir_size_bytes(dir: string) -> int ![io] {
    let native = _dir_size_bytes_native(dir);
    if native >= 0 { return native; }
    let q = _shell_single_quote_arg(dir);
    let out = _shell_read_cmd("du -sk " + q + " 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $1}'");
    return _parse_nonneg_int(out) * 1024;
}

// `dir`'s total size in bytes via `sfn_fs_stat_size`, summed over every
// regular file the tree contains. Walks with `walk_dirs`
// (`build/fs_tree.sfn`), which already bounds the traversal
// (`FS_TREE_MAX_DEPTH`/`FS_TREE_MAX_NODES`) rather than reimplementing a
// guarded BFS here. A single native read failing anywhere — including
// every read on POSIX, where the probe is an always-`-1` stub — abandons
// the walk immediately and returns `-1`, so a partial sum can never be
// reported as the total; `-1` is also returned for a file-less tree (no
// probe ever ran), so an empty directory falls back to the shell `du`
// reading exactly as it always has, rather than reporting a native `0`
// that disagrees with `du`'s minimum block accounting.
fn _dir_size_bytes_native(dir: string) -> int ![io] {
    let dirs = walk_dirs(dir, 0, 16);
    let mut total: i64 = 0;
    let mut saw_file: boolean = false;
    let mut d: int = 0;
    loop {
        if d >= dirs.length { break; }
        let entries = fs.listDirectory(dirs[d]);
        let mut e: int = 0;
        loop {
            if e >= entries.length { break; }
            let full = dirs[d] + "/" + entries[e];
            if !fs.is_directory(full) {
                let sz = sfn_fs_stat_size(full as *u8);
                if sz < 0 { return -1; }
                saw_file = true;
                total += sz;
            }
            e += 1;
        }
        d += 1;
    }
    if !saw_file { return -1; }
    return total as int;
}

// Current time in epoch seconds. Tries the native runtime clock first
// (`sfn_clock_unix_millis`, real on every target) on a Windows host only
// — POSIX stays on the pre-existing shell reading unconditionally, so its
// behaviour is byte-identical to before SFN-754, matching
// `_entry_mtime_epoch`/`_dir_size_bytes` above.
//
// All three moved together, for the reason this comment used to warn
// about: de-shelling the mtime/size reads alone while `now` stayed
// shell-bound (returning 0 on Windows) would have switched
// `cache_prune`'s `now > 0` age-eviction guard off — now that `now` is
// real too, that guard is live, so a real mtime is exactly what it needs.
// POSIX shells all three exactly as before, so the invariant holds there
// too: the age axis and the mtime/size reads move in lockstep on every
// host, never independently.
fn _now_epoch() -> int ![io] {
    if host_is_windows() {
        let mut millis: i64 = 0;
        let status = sfn_clock_unix_millis(& millis);
        if status != 0 && millis > 0 { return (millis / 1000) as int; }
    }
    return _parse_nonneg_int(_shell_read_cmd("date +%s"));
}

// Recursive delete guarded against the empty path and `/` (mirrors
// cache_clean); both guards live in `remove_tree`.
fn _rm_rf_dir(dir: string) -> boolean ![io] { return remove_tree(dir); }

// Best-effort removal of now-empty prefix shards (and any empty entry
// dirs) so `cache info` entry counts stay honest after a prune.
fn _prune_empty_shards(root: string) ![io] {
    let _ = remove_empty_dirs_under(root);
}

// Index permutation that orders `mtimes` ascending (oldest first).
// Insertion sort — entry counts are small and this runs only on the
// eviction path. Stable, so equal-mtime entries keep enumeration order.
fn _order_by_mtime_asc(mtimes: int[]) -> int[] {
    let mut order: int[] = [];
    let mut i: int = 0;
    loop {
        if i >= mtimes.length { break; }
        order.push(i);
        i += 1;
    }
    let mut k: int = 1;
    loop {
        if k >= order.length { break; }
        let cur = order[k];
        let cur_m = mtimes[cur];
        let mut j: int = k - 1;
        loop {
            if j < 0 { break; }
            if mtimes[order[j]] <= cur_m { break; }
            order[j + 1] = order[j];
            j -= 1;
        }
        order[j + 1] = cur;
        k += 1;
    }
    return order;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// `sfn cache info`
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
struct CacheInfo {
    root: string;
    entry_count: int;
    total_bytes: int;
}

// Resolved root, live entry count, and total on-disk size (bytes). A
// non-existent root reports zero/zero rather than erroring — `info` on
// a machine that has never built is a valid, empty answer.
fn cache_info(root: string) -> CacheInfo ![io] {
    let dirs = _cache_entry_dirs(root);
    let mut total: int = 0;
    if fs.exists(root) {
        let q = _shell_single_quote_arg(root);
        let out = _shell_read_cmd("du -sk " + q
            + " 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $1}'");
        total = _parse_nonneg_int(out) * 1024;
    }
    return CacheInfo {
        root: root,
        entry_count: dirs.length,
        total_bytes: total
    };
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// `sfn cache prune`
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
struct PruneResult {
    removed_entries: int;
    removed_bytes: int;
}

// LRU eviction. `max_age_days >= 0` removes any entry whose mtime is
// older than that many days; `max_size_bytes >= 0` then deletes
// oldest-first until the total falls to or below the cap (so
// `--max-size 0` empties the cache). A negative bound disables that
// axis. Age is applied before size so an aged-out entry never counts
// toward the size total. mtime is a true recency signal because a hit
// touches the entry (`cache_touch_entry`).
fn cache_prune(root: string, max_size_bytes: int, max_age_days: int) -> PruneResult ![io] {
    let mut removed_entries: int = 0;
    let mut removed_bytes: int = 0;
    if !fs.exists(root) {
        return PruneResult { removed_entries: 0, removed_bytes: 0 };
    }
    let dirs = _cache_entry_dirs(root);
    let now = _now_epoch();

    // Clamp the age axis: a `--max-age` past ~10,000 years is treated as
    // "unbounded" (no age eviction). This guards `max_age_days * 86400`
    // against i64 overflow, which would otherwise wrap negative and
    // invert the comparison into evicting *every* entry — the opposite
    // of the "keep everything" intent behind a very large value.
    let age_active = max_age_days >= 0 && max_age_days <= 3650000;

    let mut kept_dirs: string[] = [];
    let mut kept_mtimes: int[] = [];
    let mut kept_sizes: int[] = [];
    let mut i: int = 0;
    loop {
        if i >= dirs.length { break; }
        let d = dirs[i];
        let mt = _entry_mtime_epoch(d);
        let sz = _dir_size_bytes(d);
        let aged_out = age_active && now > 0 && (now - mt) > max_age_days * 86400;
        if aged_out {
            if _rm_rf_dir(d) {
                removed_entries += 1;
                removed_bytes += sz;
            }
        } else {
            kept_dirs.push(d);
            kept_mtimes.push(mt);
            kept_sizes.push(sz);
        }
        i += 1;
    }

    if max_size_bytes >= 0 {
        let mut total: int = 0;
        let mut s: int = 0;
        loop {
            if s >= kept_sizes.length { break; }
            total += kept_sizes[s];
            s += 1;
        }
        if total > max_size_bytes {
            let order = _order_by_mtime_asc(kept_mtimes);
            let mut o: int = 0;
            loop {
                if o >= order.length { break; }
                if total <= max_size_bytes { break; }
                let idx = order[o];
                if _rm_rf_dir(kept_dirs[idx]) {
                    removed_entries += 1;
                    removed_bytes += kept_sizes[idx];
                    total -= kept_sizes[idx];
                }
                o += 1;
            }
        }
    }

    _prune_empty_shards(root);
    return PruneResult {
        removed_entries: removed_entries,
        removed_bytes: removed_bytes
    };
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Stale-schema sweep (`sfn cache clean --all-schemas`)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Remove sibling `<base>/v<M>` trees for `M < N`, where `root` is
// `<base>/v<N>`. Explicit-only — invoked from `sfn cache clean
// --all-schemas`, never eagerly on a build (SFEP-0040 §3.3/§6 default:
// no eager sweep). Skips the current schema, any `v<M>` with `M >= N`,
// and every non-`v<digits>` sibling (e.g. the `test-bin/` cache), so a
// stale-schema sweep can never evict a live or unrelated tree.
fn cache_sweep_stale_schemas(root: string) -> int ![io] {
    let base = _dirname_cmd(root);
    let cur_num = _parse_schema_num(_basename_cmd(root));
    if cur_num < 0 { return 0; }
    // Unlike `_cache_entry_dirs`, this walk wants every directory, empty
    // ones included: nothing reaps them afterwards, because
    // `_prune_empty_shards` runs against `root` (`<base>/v<N>`), never
    // against `base`. An already-empty `<base>/v<M>` used to survive the
    // sweep uncounted — ~4 KiB of cruft per stale-but-empty schema —
    // because the walk could not see it. SFN-756 gave `walk_dirs` a real
    // `fs.is_directory` classifier, so it is now swept like any other.
    let entries = list_child_dirs(base);
    let mut removed: int = 0;
    let mut i: int = 0;
    loop {
        if i >= entries.length { break; }
        let n = _parse_schema_num(_basename_cmd(entries[i]));
        if n >= 0 && n < cur_num {
            if _rm_rf_dir(entries[i]) { removed += 1; }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return removed;
}

// Parse `v<digits>` -> the integer schema number; -1 when `name` is not
// exactly `v` followed by one or more decimal digits (rejects
// `test-bin`, `v2x`, bare `v`, and any tmp sibling).
fn _parse_schema_num(name: string) -> int {
    if name.length < 2 { return -1; }
    if substring(name, 0, 1) != "v" { return -1; }
    let mut val: int = 0;
    let mut i: int = 1;
    loop {
        if i >= name.length { break; }
        let code = char_code_at_text(name, i);
        if code < 48 || code > 57 { return -1; }
        val = val * 10 + (code - 48);
        i += 1;
    }
    return val;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Cache root resolution
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Pure resolution ladder (SFEP-0040 §3.1). Highest precedence first:
//   1. `explicit` ($SAILFIN_BUILD_CACHE_DIR) — CI/sandbox override.
//   2. compiler self-host pin — in-tree `build/cache` stays hermetic
//      so `make compile` / `make check` never read a developer's
//      global store (explicit still wins above, so CI can redirect
//      even the self-host cache).
//   3. `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/sailfin` — global default when XDG is set.
//   4. `$HOME/.cache/sailfin` — global default on Linux/macOS.
//   5. `build/cache` — hermetic fallback when `$HOME` is unresolvable
//      (containers, minimal CI).
// All inputs are explicit so the ladder is unit-testable without any
// process-global env mutation. The `<version>` schema suffix is
// appended uniformly across every branch.
fn cache_root_from(explicit: string, is_selfhost: boolean, xdg: string, home: string) -> string {
    let schema = build_cache_schema_version();
    if explicit.length > 0 { return explicit + "/" + schema; }
    if is_selfhost { return "build/cache/" + schema; }
    if xdg.length > 0 { return xdg + "/sailfin/" + schema; }
    if home.length > 0 { return home + "/.cache/sailfin/" + schema; }
    return "build/cache/" + schema;
}

// Pure variant — used by tests and by other callers that already hold
// an override. `override_path` empty → in-tree default
// `build/cache/<version>`; non-empty → `<override>/<version>`.
// Equivalent to the non-self-host, no-XDG/HOME slice of the ladder.
fn cache_root_with_override(override_path: string) -> string {
    return cache_root_from(override_path, false, "", "");
}

// The root capsule's scoped identity is the self-host discriminator for
// cache placement. Keep this pure so the manifest rename is directly pinned
// without depending on the process environment's cache overrides.
fn is_compiler_selfhost_capsule(capsule_name: string) -> boolean {
    return strings_equal(capsule_name, "sfn/compiler");
}

// Production reader. Resolves the cache-root ladder from the
// environment plus `capsule_name` (the current build's
// `[capsule].name`); callers should invoke once at the top of a
// build and thread the result through. `capsule_name == "sfn/compiler"`
// pins the in-tree root so the compiler self-host build stays
// hermetic (SFEP-0040 §3.1).
fn cache_root(capsule_name: string) -> string ![io] {
    let explicit = _get_env_cmd("SAILFIN_BUILD_CACHE_DIR");
    let is_selfhost = is_compiler_selfhost_capsule(capsule_name);
    let xdg = _get_env_cmd("XDG_CACHE_HOME");
    let home = _get_env_cmd("HOME");
    return cache_root_from(explicit, is_selfhost, xdg, home);
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Path layout
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Maps an artifact `kind` to its filename within an entry
// directory. Returns "" for unknown kinds (caller responsibility
// to validate inputs); this avoids panicking for forward-compat
// when a new artifact kind is added.
fn _filename_for_kind(kind: string) -> string {
    if kind == "sfn-asm" { return "ir.sfn-asm"; }
    if kind == "layout-manifest" { return "layout.manifest"; }
    if kind == "ll" { return "mod.ll"; }
    if kind == "obj" { return "mod.o"; }
    // Runtime C/LL/sfn object compiles (#1096). A distinct filename
    // from the per-module `.ll` `obj` kind keeps the two layers from
    // ever aliasing in a shared cache entry, even though their content
    // keys are already disjoint.
    if kind == "runtime-obj" { return "runtime.o"; }
    // Per-test linked binary cache (#1230, Lever 2 of
    // `docs/proposals/0011-ci-test-speed.md`). A distinct filename keeps
    // the test-binary layer from ever aliasing the per-module `.o`
    // `obj` kind in a shared entry directory, mirroring the
    // `runtime-obj` precedent.
    if kind == "test-bin" { return "test.bin"; }
    return "";
}

// `<root>/<key[0..2]>/<key>/<filename>`. Pure.
fn cache_artifact_path(root: string, key: CacheKey, kind: string) -> string {
    let prefix = substring(key.digest, 0, 2);
    let filename = _filename_for_kind(kind);
    return root + "/" + prefix + "/" + key.digest + "/" + filename;
}

fn cache_paths_for(root: string, key: CacheKey) -> CacheEntryPaths {
    return CacheEntryPaths {
        sfn_asm: cache_artifact_path(root, key, "sfn-asm"),
        layout_manifest: cache_artifact_path(root, key, "layout-manifest"),
        ll: cache_artifact_path(root, key, "ll"),
        obj: cache_artifact_path(root, key, "obj")
    };
}

// `<root>/<key[0..2]>/<key>` — the per-entry directory.
fn _cache_entry_dir(root: string, key: CacheKey) -> string {
    let prefix = substring(key.digest, 0, 2);
    return root + "/" + prefix + "/" + key.digest;
}

// True iff `digest` is a 64-character lowercase hex string —
// exactly the shape sha256 produces. Empty / short / non-hex
// digests appear when `_sha256_of_file` fails (source file missing,
// unreadable input, allocation failure, or size-cap refusal).
// Cache reads/writes against an invalid digest would land at
// nonsense paths like `<root>///ir.sfn-asm` and pollute the
// cache root with cross-module collisions; treating them as a
// hard miss is the safe default.
// Public alias of `_is_valid_digest` so the resolver can use the
// same predicate when classifying a freshly-derived `cache_key`.
// Without sharing the validator, callers might check `digest.length
// == 64` (which SHA-256 always satisfies on success) but miss
// the non-hex case (where a future hash function or an injected
// digest could leak in). One source of truth keeps
// `ModuleCacheEvent.invalid_key` honest.
fn is_valid_digest(digest: string) -> boolean {
    return _is_valid_digest(digest);
}

fn _is_valid_digest(digest: string) -> boolean {
    if digest.length != 64 { return false; }
    let mut i: int = 0;
    loop {
        if i >= digest.length { break; }
        let code = char_code_at_text(digest, i);
        let is_digit = code >= 48 && code <= 57;  // 0-9
        let is_lower_hex = code >= 97 && code <= 102;  // a-f
        if !is_digit && !is_lower_hex { return false; }
        i += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Lookup
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// True iff every artifact for `key` is present on disk. Partial
// entries (e.g. interrupted writes) are treated as misses; the
// caller should overwrite with a fresh `cache_store` rather than
// trust a half-populated directory. Invalid digests are also
// treated as a miss — see `_is_valid_digest` for rationale.
fn cache_lookup_complete(root: string, key: CacheKey) -> boolean ![io] {
    if !_is_valid_digest(key.digest) { return false; }
    let paths = cache_paths_for(root, key);
    if !fs.exists(paths.sfn_asm) { return false; }
    if !fs.exists(paths.layout_manifest) { return false; }
    if !fs.exists(paths.ll) { return false; }
    if !fs.exists(paths.obj) { return false; }
    return true;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Atomic publish
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Copy `src` to a unique temp file in the *same directory* as
// `dst`, then rename it into place. A rename within one filesystem
// is atomic, so a concurrent reader (another parallel emit worker,
// or the linker) observes `dst` as either absent or the complete
// file — never a half-written artifact.
//
// Without this, the prior in-place `cp <src> <final-path>` let a
// reader observe a truncated `mod.ll` / `ir.sfn-asm` while a peer
// worker was still writing it. That surfaces downstream as `use of
// undefined value …` (truncated `.ll`) or, when the signature
// side-companion is read mid-write, `llvm lowering [fatal]: cannot
// resolve return type for call to <X>` — the nondeterministic
// cold-cache failures tracked in #1011. The driver fans out
// parallel `<self> emit` subprocess workers (`cli_main.sfn`,
// `SAILFIN_BUILD_JOBS`) that share `build/cache/<schema>`, so the
// race fires within a single `sfn build`/`run`, not only under
// `make test`.
//
// The temp lives beside `dst` (not `/tmp`) so the rename stays on
// one filesystem; a cross-device rename would fail outright. Both
// the temp mint and the publish now share `build/fs.sfn`'s helpers
// rather than the duplicate inline `mktemp`/`cp`/`mv` sequence this
// once carried (SFN-490) — that duplicate was a distinct code path
// that SFN-478's Windows-host work never reached. When no temp can
// be minted the helper degrades to a direct in-place copy: atomicity
// is lost but the build still produces the artifact.
fn _cache_atomic_copy(src: string, dst: string) -> boolean ![io] {
    let tmp = _mktemp_sibling_cmd(dst);
    if tmp.length == 0 {
        // No temp available — degrade to a non-atomic direct copy.
        return _copy_file(src, dst);
    }
    if !_copy_file(src, tmp) {
        fs.deleteFile(tmp);
        return false;
    }
    return _atomic_rename_into_place(tmp, dst);
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Store
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Copies the four source artifacts into the cache. Returns true
// on success, false if any copy fails (caller should treat as a
// non-fatal cache miss — the build itself still succeeded with
// the in-tree artifacts). Invalid digests short-circuit to false
// before any filesystem mutation, so a hash failure cannot
// pollute the cache root with bogus-path entries.
fn cache_store(root: string, key: CacheKey, sfn_asm_src: string, layout_manifest_src: string, ll_src: string, obj_src: string) -> boolean ![io] {
    if !_is_valid_digest(key.digest) { return false; }
    let entry_dir = _cache_entry_dir(root, key);
    _ensure_dir_recursive_cmd(entry_dir);
    let paths = cache_paths_for(root, key);
    if !_cache_atomic_copy(sfn_asm_src, paths.sfn_asm) { return false; }
    if !_cache_atomic_copy(layout_manifest_src, paths.layout_manifest) {
        return false;
    }
    if !_cache_atomic_copy(ll_src, paths.ll) { return false; }
    if !_cache_atomic_copy(obj_src, paths.obj) { return false; }
    return true;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Single-artifact lookup / store / restore
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Build stages typically produce only a subset of the four
// artifact kinds. `_cr_compile_one` in `capsule_resolver.sfn`,
// for instance, produces only `.ll` — the `.sfn-asm` and
// `.layout-manifest` are produced earlier by
// `stage_capsule_imports`, and the `.o` is produced downstream by
// clang. The single-artifact API lets a stage cache exactly what
// it produced without needing to invent placeholders for the
// other slots.
//
// `kind` must be one of the four recognised values
// (`"sfn-asm"`, `"layout-manifest"`, `"ll"`, `"obj"`); unknown
// kinds short-circuit to false the same way invalid digests do.
fn _is_known_kind(kind: string) -> boolean {
    return _filename_for_kind(kind).length > 0;
}

// True iff the artifact of `kind` for `key` is on disk under
// `root`. Returns false on invalid digest or unknown kind so the
// caller can treat both as a hard miss.
fn cache_lookup_artifact(root: string, key: CacheKey, kind: string) -> boolean ![io] {
    if !_is_valid_digest(key.digest) { return false; }
    if !_is_known_kind(kind) { return false; }
    return fs.exists(cache_artifact_path(root, key, kind));
}

// Copy a cached artifact out to `dst_path`. Returns true on
// success, false on missing entry, invalid key, unknown kind, or
// `cp` failure. Used on cache hits to materialise the cached
// artifact into the location the build expects (e.g. copying a
// cached `.ll` to the `CapsuleSource.ll_path` the linker reads).
//
// The destination's parent directory is created on demand —
// without that, `cp` would fail with "No such file or directory"
// on the first build into a fresh tree, silently flipping every
// cache hit into a recompile.
fn cache_copy_artifact_to(root: string, key: CacheKey, kind: string, dst_path: string) -> boolean ![io] {
    let ok = _cache_copy_artifact_to_impl(root, key, kind, dst_path);
    // Cache hit: bump the entry's mtime so `cache_prune` sees true
    // recency (SFEP-0040 §3.4). Best-effort — never fails the restore.
    if ok { let _ = cache_touch_entry(root, key); }
    return ok;
}

// Same restore as `cache_copy_artifact_to`, but skips the recency-bumping
// `touch`. For a caller restoring MULTIPLE artifacts out of the SAME entry
// directory in one operation (the staging cache's `.sfn-asm` +
// `.layout-manifest` pair, e.g.) — one entry needs one recency bump, not
// one per artifact, and `touch` forks a process per call.
fn cache_copy_artifact_to_untouched(root: string, key: CacheKey, kind: string, dst_path: string) -> boolean ![io] {
    return _cache_copy_artifact_to_impl(root, key, kind, dst_path);
}

fn _cache_copy_artifact_to_impl(root: string, key: CacheKey, kind: string, dst_path: string) -> boolean ![io] {
    if !_is_valid_digest(key.digest) { return false; }
    if !_is_known_kind(kind) { return false; }
    let src = cache_artifact_path(root, key, kind);
    if !fs.exists(src) { return false; }
    let dst_dir = _dirname_cmd(dst_path);
    if dst_dir.length > 0 { _ensure_dir_recursive_cmd(dst_dir); }
    return _cache_atomic_copy(src, dst_path);
}

// Copy `src_path` into the cache as the artifact of `kind` for
// `key`. Creates the entry directory on demand. Returns true on
// success, false on invalid key, unknown kind, or `cp` failure
// (caller treats as a non-fatal cache write miss — the build
// itself still produced the artifact at `src_path`).
fn cache_store_artifact(root: string, key: CacheKey, kind: string, src_path: string) -> boolean ![io] {
    if !_is_valid_digest(key.digest) { return false; }
    if !_is_known_kind(kind) { return false; }
    let entry_dir = _cache_entry_dir(root, key);
    _ensure_dir_recursive_cmd(entry_dir);
    let dst = cache_artifact_path(root, key, kind);
    let stored = _cache_atomic_copy(src_path, dst);
    if stored {
        // SFN-148: debug-gated emit ledger. A `.ll` store means a cache
        // miss→emit just happened; recording the key digest lets a
        // regression assert each dep module is emitted at most once per
        // invocation (a duplicate digest == a stampede). Inert unless
        // SAILFIN_EMIT_LEDGER is set, so real builds pay nothing.
        if strings_equal(kind, "ll") {
            let ledger = _get_env_cmd("SAILFIN_EMIT_LEDGER");
            if ledger.length > 0 {
                if !fs.appendFile(ledger, key.digest + "\n") { return false; }
            }
        }
    }
    return stored;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Hashing helpers
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Hash a string in-process via the vendored SHA-256 (#1995,
// `docs/proposals/draft-test-runner-invocation-cache.md` §3-B).
// `unique_suffix` is accepted for API compatibility but is unused:
// the in-process path has no shared tmp file and therefore no race
// (the mktemp workaround is now moot).
fn _sha256_of_string(input: string, unique_suffix: string) -> string ![io] {
    return sha256_hex_of_string(input);
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// String comparison + sort (for dep-hash canonicalization)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Lexicographic less-than. Operates on raw character codes; used
// for sorting hex digests (`[0-9a-f]+`) and JSON-string flag
// values where this is a faithful byte-wise compare.
fn _string_lt(a: string, b: string) -> boolean {
    let mut i: int = 0;
    loop {
        if i >= a.length || i >= b.length { break; }
        let ca = char_code_at_text(a, i);
        let cb = char_code_at_text(b, i);
        if ca < cb { return true; }
        if ca > cb { return false; }
        i += 1;
    }
    return a.length < b.length;
}

// Insertion sort — N is small (per-module dep counts are typically
// <100, and the compiler itself has 132 modules total).
fn _sort_strings(input: string[]) -> string[] {
    let mut out: string[] = [];
    let mut copy_i: int = 0;
    loop {
        if copy_i >= input.length { break; }
        out.push(input[copy_i]);
        copy_i += 1;
    }
    let mut k: int = 1;
    loop {
        if k >= out.length { break; }
        let cur = out[k];
        let mut j: int = k - 1;
        loop {
            if j < 0 { break; }
            if !_string_lt(cur, out[j]) { break; }
            out[j + 1] = out[j];
            j -= 1;
        }
        out[j + 1] = cur;
        k += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Flag canonicalization
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// JSON string escaper. Handles the characters that would otherwise
// break the array shape (`"`, `\`) plus the three control-character
// escapes Sailfin's string-literal decoder recognises (`\n`, `\r`,
// `\t`, see `compiler/capsules/syntax/src/parser/utils.sfn::decode_string_literal_escapes`).
// Without this, a flag containing a newline would both produce
// invalid JSON AND collide with the `\n` separator we use between
// cache-key tuple fields, breaking the "separator prevents
// concatenation collisions" invariant.
//
// `\b` and `\f` are intentionally excluded: Sailfin source cannot
// produce them in a string literal, so they cannot reach this
// encoder via any in-language path. If a future caller surfaces a
// flag containing one (e.g. through OS-level argv with a raw control
// byte), we'll extend this branch and add a unit test that builds
// the character via `char_code` rather than a source literal.
fn _escape_json_string(input: string) -> string {
    let mut out: string = "";
    let mut i: int = 0;
    loop {
        if i >= input.length { break; }
        let ch = char_at(input, i);
        let code = char_code_at_text(input, i);
        if ch == "\\" { out = out + "\\\\"; } else if ch == "\"" {
            out = out + "\\\"";
        } else if code == 9 { out = out + "\\t"; } else if code == 10 {
            out = out + "\\n";
        } else if code == 13 { out = out + "\\r"; } else { out = out + ch; }
        i += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// Sort the flag list and JSON-encode as a string array. Order
// invariance means `["-O0", "--release"]` and `["--release", "-O0"]`
// produce the same canonical form, hence the same cache key.
fn canonicalize_flags(flags: string[]) -> string {
    let sorted = _sort_strings(flags);
    let mut out: string = "[";
    let mut i: int = 0;
    loop {
        if i >= sorted.length { break; }
        if i > 0 { out = out + ","; }
        out = out + "\"" + _escape_json_string(sorted[i]) + "\"";
        i += 1;
    }
    return out + "]";
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Content-hash primitive (shared)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Content-address any file through the shared binary-safe in-process path.
// Returns "" if the file does not exist or cannot be read — callers must
// treat an empty hash as "not
// content-addressable" and fall back to recompile rather than risk a stale
// hit (#514, #632).
fn sha256_of_file(file_path: string) -> string ![io] {
    return _sha256_of_file(file_path);
}

// Content-addressed cache key for a runtime C / LL object compile.
// The runtime-capsule and legacy runtime-object compile paths in
// `cli_main.sfn` previously keyed their `.o` cache purely on the
// source basename + opt flag, so editing a tracked source left a
// stale cached object that the linker silently reused (#632). This
// helper mixes the source file's content SHA-256 with the opt flag
// so a content edit busts the key while a pure mtime touch (same
// bytes) keeps it stable. The `\n` separator keeps the two fields
// from colliding via concatenation, matching `cache_key_for`'s
// invariant. Returns "" when the source can't be hashed so the
// caller can fall back to an always-recompile path.
fn runtime_object_cache_key(src_path: string, opt_flag: string) -> string ![io] {
    let content = sha256_of_file(src_path);
    if content.length == 0 { return ""; }
    // The `secsplit1` tag pins the compile-flag scheme into the key.
    // #1047 added `-ffunction-sections -fdata-sections` to every runtime
    // object compile so the final link's `--gc-sections` can strip per
    // function; an object cached by an earlier compiler lacks those
    // sections, and silently reusing it would defeat the strip (coarse
    // `.text`) and break the runtime-root retention. Bumping the tag
    // forces a one-time recompile so cached objects match the link flags.
    //
    // M1.A.2 (string `i8*` -> `{i8*, i64}` aggregate flip): the runtime
    // object key hashes only the *source* + opt-flag, not the compiler's
    // codegen ABI. The string-ABI flip changed the emitted signatures of
    // prelude string functions (`find_char`, `string_starts_with`,
    // `char_code`, ...) from `i8*` to the two-word `{i8*, i64}` aggregate
    // without touching their source, so a runtime object compiled by a
    // pre-flip compiler (or restored from CI's `actions/cache`, or staged
    // by the seed during bootstrap) was silently reused — its `i8*`
    // prelude then mismatched the flipped `{i8*, i64}` call sites emitted
    // by the new compiler, so e.g. `find_char` read its 2nd string arg out
    // of the 1st string's length register and returned garbage (the
    // `sailfin run` HTTP-consumer `req.path` came back empty -> 404; see
    // test_http_capsule_serve.sh). Bump the tag to `strabi1` so every
    // runtime object recompiles once under the aggregate ABI; future
    // codegen-ABI changes that the source hash cannot see must bump it
    // again (same contract as the secsplit bump above).
    return content + "\n" + opt_flag + "\nsecsplit1-strabi1";
}

// Compiler-identity-aware variant of `runtime_object_cache_key` for the
// runtime sfn-source emit path (#1197).
//
// `runtime_object_cache_key` keys a runtime object purely on its source
// content + opt flag + a hand-maintained ABI tag. That is correct for the
// C / LL object path (`clang -c` output does not depend on the Sailfin
// compiler's codegen), but it is UNSOUND for runtime `.sfn` sources, which
// are emitted to `.ll`/`.o` BY the Sailfin compiler: a codegen change in
// `compiler/src` alters the emitted IR of a runtime module WITHOUT touching
// the module's source bytes, so the source-only key hits and the linker
// silently reuses a stale object emitted by the previous compiler. That is
// exactly the failure #1197 documents (a cast-lowering fix never reaching the
// linked `future.sfn` runtime until a manual `rm`), and the C-runtime
// deletion (#822/#823) made it the common case — the *entire* runtime now
// flows through this compiler-emitted path. The historical mitigation was to
// hand-bump the `secsplit1-strabi1` tag on every codegen-ABI change; folding
// the compiler identity in makes that automatic.
//
// `compiler_identity` is the same value the `.ll` module cache mixes into
// `cache_key_for` — `cache_compiler_identity(version, binary_path)`: the
// build stamp (a committed codegen change bumps the `+dev.<hash>` commit
// hash) plus, for `.dirty` stamps, the emitting binary's SHA-256 (an
// iterative rebuild at the same commit busts the key). The determinism gate's
// pass1→pass2 use the SAME binary, so the identity — and therefore the key —
// is stable across those work-dirs and the shared cache still warm-hits.
//
// Returns "" when the base key is unhashable (propagating the
// always-recompile fallback). An empty `compiler_identity` degrades to the
// base key byte-for-byte (no worse than the pre-#1197 behaviour).
fn runtime_object_cache_key_with_identity(src_path: string, opt_flag: string, compiler_identity: string) -> string ![io] {
    let base = runtime_object_cache_key(src_path, opt_flag);
    if base.length == 0 { return ""; }
    if compiler_identity.length == 0 { return base; }
    return base + "\n" + compiler_identity;
}

// Derive a content-addressed shared-cache `CacheKey` from a
// `runtime_object_cache_key` string (#1096). The runtime object layer
// historically cached `.o` outputs only in the per-build work-dir (a
// sidecar `.o.key` next to the object in `<work-dir>/sailfin/`), so a
// second build into a *sibling* work-dir — e.g. the build-quality
// determinism gate's pass1 → pass2 — always missed every runtime
// object, dragging the folded summary `hit_rate` below the 0.95 gate
// even though nothing changed. Hashing the runtime key string into a
// 64-hex digest lets the shared-cache artifact helpers
// (`cache_lookup_artifact` / `cache_store_artifact`, kind
// `"runtime-obj"`) back the work-dir objects with the same
// content-addressed `build/cache/<schema>` store the `.ll` module
// cache already uses, so runtime objects warm across work-dirs and CI
// cache restores. Returns an empty-digest key (rejected by
// `_is_valid_digest`, so every shared-cache helper treats it as a
// miss) when the runtime key was empty/unhashable.
fn runtime_object_entry_key_from_str(key_str: string, hash_suffix: string) -> CacheKey ![io] {
    if key_str.length == 0 {
        return CacheKey { digest: "" };
    }
    return CacheKey { digest: _sha256_of_string(key_str, hash_suffix) };
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Cache key derivation
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Compute the content-addressed cache key for a single module
// compile. See top-of-file contract for the formula.
//
// Preconditions (caller responsibility):
//   - `source_path` exists and is readable.
//   - Every entry in `dep_layout_manifest_paths` exists and is
//     readable.
//   - `dep_slugs` is parallel to `dep_layout_manifest_paths`:
//     `dep_slugs[i]` is the slug of the module that produced
//     `dep_layout_manifest_paths[i]`. A length mismatch is a caller
//     bug and yields an EMPTY digest, which every cache helper
//     rejects as a hard miss so the module recompiles.
//
// The mismatch path fails safe deliberately. Substituting an empty
// slug for the missing entries would reconstruct the exact SFN-661
// collision — two layout-free providers would both contribute
// `"" + "\n" + <empty-manifest-hash>` and become indistinguishable
// again. A caller bug must degrade to "recompile everything", never
// to "a weaker key", because the weaker key is the failure this
// function exists to prevent.
//
// The dep slug — not the manifest path — is what enters the digest.
// Manifest paths are absolute and contain the per-invocation import
// context root, so hashing them would make keys unshareable across
// runners and work-dirs, breaking the CI cache restore the whole
// content-addressing scheme exists to serve.
//
// Failures in `_sha256_of_file` (missing or unreadable input) collapse to
// empty digests; the resulting key is still
// stable but reflects "input was missing", which the caller can
// surface as a build setup error.
//
// `slug` (schema v2) is the per-enumeration module slug — the
// driver passes `src.slug` (e.g. a name-shaped `sfn/cli/mod` or a
// path-shaped slug derived from a relative import). It is mixed into
// the digest as a fifth input so the same source enumerated under two
// distinct slugs cannot collide on one cache entry (see top-of-file
// contract and #527). Because it feeds the digest it must be
// *stable* for a given module enumeration — it is NOT a per-worker
// nonce; a slug that varied per worker would give the same module a
// different key each run and defeat cache hits. Distinct enumerations carry
// distinct slugs, preventing concurrent workers from sharing an identity.
fn cache_key_for(source_path: string, dep_slugs: string[], dep_layout_manifest_paths: string[], compiler_version: string, flags_canonical: string, slug: string, target_triple: string) -> CacheKey ![io] {
    // A length mismatch means the caller built the two arrays
    // independently and they drifted. Fail safe: an empty digest is
    // rejected by `_is_valid_digest`, so every lookup and store treats
    // it as a miss and the module recompiles (SFN-661).
    if dep_slugs.length != dep_layout_manifest_paths.length {
        return CacheKey { digest: "" };
    }

    let s_hash = _sha256_of_file(source_path);

    let mut dep_hashes: string[] = [];
    let mut i: int = 0;
    loop {
        if i >= dep_layout_manifest_paths.length { break; }
        // `<dep slug>\n<manifest content hash>` — the provider's
        // identity and its interface bytes, so a dependent's key moves
        // when either changes (SFN-661). `\n` separates the two so a
        // slug ending in hex cannot collide with a shorter slug plus a
        // longer hash.
        dep_hashes.push(dep_slugs[i] + "\n" + _sha256_of_file(dep_layout_manifest_paths[i]));
        i += 1;
    }
    let sorted_dep_hashes = _sort_strings(dep_hashes);
    let mut deps_concat: string = "";
    let mut j: int = 0;
    loop {
        if j >= sorted_dep_hashes.length { break; }
        if j > 0 { deps_concat = deps_concat + "|"; }
        deps_concat = deps_concat + sorted_dep_hashes[j];
        j += 1;
    }
    let deps_hash = _sha256_of_string(deps_concat, slug + "_deps");

    // SFN-677: fold the build TARGET in, under a bumpable scheme tag,
    // matching `_runtime_obj_key_with_target` (`build/runtime_objs.sfn`,
    // SFN-617) and the `toolchain_key` fold in `build/clang_argv.sfn`.
    //
    // SFN-774 / SFEP-0068 §3.6: promoted from `target_os` to the TRIPLE,
    // with the marker bumped `tgt1:` -> `tgt2:` so a pre-promotion entry
    // (keyed on the coarser OS string) can never collide with a
    // post-promotion one. The OS alone could not distinguish the two
    // Windows ABIs (`x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` vs `x86_64-w64-mingw32`, both
    // `"Windows"`), so an msvc build and a mingw build would otherwise
    // silently share a cache entry despite compiling to different objects
    // — the SFN-677 defect, one axis wider.
    //
    // `target_triple` goes LAST and nothing derived from it follows. That
    // ordering is load-bearing, not cosmetic. It may originate from an
    // unvalidated `SAILFIN_TARGET_TRIPLE`/`SAILFIN_TARGET_OS` env string,
    // so it may contain any byte, including the separators used here;
    // appending anything after it lets a crafted value absorb its own
    // successor and alias a different tuple. Concretely, an earlier draft
    // also folded `target_clang_flags(target_os)` space-joined after it,
    // which made `"Windows"` (flags `-target x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`) and
    // the literal `"Windows -target x86_64-pc-windows-msvc"` (no flags,
    // since `target_os_is_windows` is an exact match) hash identically
    // while `llvm/lowering_debug_state.sfn` emitted different IR for each
    // — reintroducing this very bug for one input pair.
    //
    // Those flags were also irrelevant to this cache: it stores compiler-
    // emitted module IR, while clang target flags affect the later IR-to-object
    // assembly cache. SFN-1117 made the flags depend on the host triple to
    // distinguish native from cross invocations; that host input is folded by
    // the object caches that consume clang, not by this IR key.
    // SFN-923: marker bumped `tgt2:` -> `tgt3:`. This is NOT cosmetic. The
    // key folds the PARENT-resolved triple, but before SFN-923 the artifact
    // stored under it could have been emitted by a HOST-flavoured emit
    // child (the `--target` process cell never reached per-module emit
    // subprocesses), so a Windows-keyed entry could legitimately hold Linux
    // IR. Those poisoned entries never self-heal — they invalidate only on
    // a `cache_compiler_identity` change, which never happens for a fixed
    // released seed. Without this bump, anyone who attempted a cross build
    // before the SFN-923 fix keeps a poisoned `tgt2:` entry that silently
    // defeats it even after upgrading.
    let final_input = s_hash + "\n" + deps_hash + "\n" + compiler_version + "\n"
        + flags_canonical
        + "\n"
        + slug
        + "\ntgt3:"
        + target_triple;
    let final_digest = _sha256_of_string(final_input, slug + "_final");
    return CacheKey { digest: final_digest };
}

// Cache key for the STAGING artifacts (`.sfn-asm` + `.layout-manifest`),
// distinct from `cache_key_for`'s per-module `.ll`/`.o` key.
//
// Omits the dep-manifest hashes `cache_key_for` folds in: native
// `.sfn-asm` emit consults no cross-module import context —
// `write_staged_native_text_file` (`native_artifact_writer.sfn`) passes
// `empty_import_symbols()`, and the subprocess path
// (`stage_native_text_with_retry`) passes no `--import-context` flag in
// native mode — so a staged module's bytes are a pure function of its own
// source text, slug, and emitting compiler. They are also not available
// here: staging runs BEFORE dep manifests exist (staging PRODUCES the
// manifests that `cache_key_for` later consumes).
//
// Includes `target_triple` even though native emit is believed
// target-neutral. Deliberately conservative: an over-specific key costs a
// duplicate cache entry per target, but an under-specific key risks
// serving one target's bytes to another — revisit only with evidence the
// emit is provably target-neutral.
//
// `stage1:` is a domain separator, distinct from `cache_key_for`'s
// `tgt3:`, so the two key spaces can never collide even for the same
// source/slug/version/triple tuple.
// Hashes through `sha256_of_file` — the SAME entry point the `.srchash`
// sidecar uses (`_cr_write_srchash`). That identity is load-bearing, not
// incidental: `stage_cache_key_from_digest` lets a caller reuse one digest
// for both the key and the sidecar, which is only sound while the two
// derive from the same function. Keep them on one entry point rather than
// two that merely happen to agree.
fn stage_cache_key_for(source_path: string, slug: string, compiler_version: string, target_triple: string) -> CacheKey ![io] {
    let s_hash = sha256_of_file(source_path);
    return stage_cache_key_from_digest(s_hash, slug, compiler_version, target_triple);
}

// Same derivation as `stage_cache_key_for`, but takes an ALREADY-COMPUTED
// source digest instead of hashing `source_path` itself. A caller that has
// already hashed the source for another purpose (the local `.srchash`
// sidecar, in particular) uses this to avoid a second file read + SHA-256
// pass for one staging publish (SFN-861 follow-up). `stage_cache_key_for`
// delegates here so the two can never drift on the derivation formula.
fn stage_cache_key_from_digest(s_hash: string, slug: string, compiler_version: string, target_triple: string) -> CacheKey ![io] {
    if s_hash.length == 0 {
        return CacheKey { digest: "" };
    }
    let final_input = s_hash + "\n" + compiler_version + "\n" + slug
        + "\nstage1:"
        + target_triple;
    let final_digest = _sha256_of_string(final_input, slug + "_stage");
    return CacheKey { digest: final_digest };
}

fn _ends_with_suffix(text: string, suffix: string) -> boolean {
    if suffix.length > text.length { return false; }
    return substring(text, text.length - suffix.length, text.length) == suffix;
}

// Augment the raw `compiler_version` cache-key component with the
// emitting compiler binary's content hash when — and only when — the
// build stamp is `.dirty`.
//
// Why: the cache key uses `compiler_version` as its sole
// compiler-identity input (§4.12), trusting the build stamp to change
// whenever the compiler's codegen changes. That holds for tagged
// (`<version>`) and clean-commit (`<version>+dev.<hash>`) stamps —
// both reproducible from the binary's provenance — but NOT for
// `<version>+dev.<hash>.dirty`: `.dirty` is a bare boolean
// (`build_stamp.sfn`), so every uncommitted working tree at the same
// commit produces the identical stamp. During iterative compiler
// development that means a `.ll` emitted by one dirty build is served
// to a DIFFERENT dirty build at the same commit — the cache serves
// stale codegen after a rebuild that was supposed to change it.
//
// Mixing the binary's SHA-256 into the identity for `.dirty` stamps
// makes a rebuilt compiler bust the cache (the binary content changed
// ⇒ the key changes). Tagged / clean-commit stamps are left untouched
// so their keys stay reproducible across machines and CI cache
// sharing is unaffected. The caller resolves `compiler_version` once
// per build, so the single `_sha256_of_file` here is paid once,
// not per module. An empty or unhashable `compiler_binary_path`
// falls back to the raw stamp (no worse than the prior behaviour).
fn cache_compiler_identity(compiler_version: string, compiler_binary_path: string) -> string ![io] {
    if !_ends_with_suffix(compiler_version, ".dirty") {
        return compiler_version;
    }
    if compiler_binary_path.length == 0 { return compiler_version; }
    let bin_hash = _sha256_of_file(compiler_binary_path);
    if bin_hash.length == 0 { return compiler_version; }
    return compiler_version + "+bin." + bin_hash;
}

// Content identity for the compiler binary that emits and links per-test
// executables. Unlike `cache_compiler_identity`, this ALWAYS folds the
// binary hash: the test-bin version component deliberately strips the
// per-commit `+dev.<hash>` metadata so unchanged compiler binaries can
// share entries across commits, but two different binaries at the same
// capsule version must never address the same cached executable.
//
// The binary-safe hash is resolved once per `sfn test` invocation by the
// caller. Failure
// returns an empty identity so the runner can disable cache reads and
// writes; falling back to the bare capsule version would recreate the
// stale cross-compiler hit this identity prevents.
fn test_bin_compiler_identity(compiler_version: string, compiler_binary_path: string) -> string ![io] {
    if compiler_version.length == 0 { return ""; }
    if compiler_binary_path.length == 0 { return ""; }
    let bin_hash = _sha256_of_file(compiler_binary_path);
    if bin_hash.length == 0 { return ""; }
    return compiler_version + "+bin." + bin_hash;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Per-test binary cache (#1230)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Lever 2 (variant 2a) of `docs/proposals/0011-ci-test-speed.md`: content-
// address each test's linked native executable so an unchanged test
// skips LLVM lowering + the clang link (the dominant per-test cost)
// and just re-runs the cached binary. This is a *second consumer* of
// the same content-addressing correctness model `cache_key_for` uses
// for the module IR cache — a changed input (test source, any
// transitive dep the link consumes, the compiler's identity, or the
// clang flag set) changes the key and misses the cache; it can never
// produce a false hit.
//
// The on-disk layout reuses the module cache's prefix-sharded entry
// directory (`cache_artifact_path` with the `"test-bin"` kind) under a
// SEPARATE schema-versioned root so the two layers age out
// independently. Lookups/stores/restores go through the existing
// single-artifact helpers (`cache_lookup_artifact`,
// `cache_store_artifact`, `cache_copy_artifact_to`), inheriting their
// atomic temp+rename publish and digest validation for free.
// Bumped when the test-binary key derivation or layout changes in a
// way that makes old entries incompatible. Independent of
// `build_cache_schema_version` so a module-cache schema bump doesn't
// needlessly evict warm test binaries (and vice versa).
//
// v1 -> v2 (#1233): the key gained a `runtime_identity` component (the
// runtime link inputs, hashed by content) and the `compiler_identity`
// component switched from the per-commit build stamp to the commit-stable
// capsule version (`resolve_test_bin_identity_for_cache`). Both change
// the folded key bytes, so the schema bump re-roots the on-disk tree to
// `build/cache/test-bin/v2` and old v1 entries can never false-hit.
//
// v2 -> v3 (SFN-545): `compiler_identity` gained the emitting compiler
// binary's SHA-256. The schema bump makes every v2 entry unreachable
// once; afterward broad CI prefix restores may unpack foreign v3 entries,
// but their compiler-specific key digests cannot match this binary's lookup.
//
// v3 -> v4 (SFN-676): the key gained a `target_triple` component. Before
// this, a cross-target `sfn test` invocation (e.g. building for
// `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` from a Linux host) could serve a binary that
// was linked for a DIFFERENT target, because nothing in the key
// distinguished them — the same defect `cache_key_for` fixed for the
// module `.ll` cache under SFN-677. The schema bump makes every v3
// entry unreachable once, so a pre-fix entry linked for the wrong
// target can never false-hit under the new key.
fn test_bin_cache_schema_version() -> string { return "v4"; }

// Root for the per-test binary cache: `<base>/test-bin/<schema>`,
// where `<base>` is `$SAILFIN_BUILD_CACHE_DIR` (when set) or the
// in-tree `build/cache` default. Kept beside — not under — the module
// cache's `<base>/v<N>` tree so the two schema lines evolve
// independently. Pure variant for tests.
fn test_bin_cache_root_with_override(override_path: string) -> string {
    let mut base: string = "build/cache";
    if override_path.length > 0 { base = override_path; }
    return base + "/test-bin/" + test_bin_cache_schema_version();
}

// Production reader. Resolves `$SAILFIN_BUILD_CACHE_DIR` at call time.
fn test_bin_cache_root() -> string ![io] {
    return test_bin_cache_root_with_override(_get_env_cmd("SAILFIN_BUILD_CACHE_DIR"));
}

// SFN-1086: root for the shared content-addressed runtime object cache as
// the `sfn test` link path addresses it. Deliberately the SAME store the
// build/run path uses, so a runtime object emitted by `sfn build` serves a
// later `sfn test` link and vice versa.
//
// `cache_root_with_override` is `cache_root_from(x, false, "", "")`, which
// is string-identical to `cache_root("sfn/compiler")` in both branches that
// can be reached here: an explicit `$SAILFIN_BUILD_CACHE_DIR` short-circuits
// at the top of the ladder, and otherwise the self-host pin resolves to the
// same in-tree `build/cache/<schema>`. Reusing the pure helper keeps one
// copy of the ladder rather than duplicating it for this caller.
//
// The divergence this does NOT close: for a non-self-host capsule with no
// explicit override, `cache_root` resolves to `$XDG_CACHE_HOME`/`$HOME`
// while this stays in-tree, so a user project's `sfn build` and `sfn test`
// populate different stores. Both remain persistent across invocations —
// which is the property this root exists for — they simply do not share.
// Unifying the ladder needs a capsule name the test command does not have
// (`discovery.sfn` resolves one per suite, not one per invocation).
//
// Note the in-tree default is RELATIVE, so the store is cwd-bound and
// "persists across invocations" holds only for invocations sharing a cwd.
// That is satisfied here because neither `run_capture` nor
// `spawn_with_env` chdir, so a pooled child inherits the parent's cwd, and
// both `sfn build` and `sfn test` run from the repo root in this tree.
fn runtime_obj_cache_root() -> string ![io] {
    return cache_root_with_override(_get_env_cmd("SAILFIN_BUILD_CACHE_DIR"));
}

// Derive the content-addressed cache key for a single test's linked
// binary. The dep set is the test-source-specific set of inputs the
// link consumes — the resolver-produced dependency `.ll` files
// (`dep_ll_paths`, hashed off disk) plus the staged `.sfn-asm` import
// contexts (`native_texts`, hashed in memory). Deriving the key from
// the same resolver output the link already uses is the correctness
// hinge for the test's OWN closure: a changed test source or dep
// changes a hash here exactly as it changes the bytes the linker reads.
//
// The link ALSO pulls in the assembled runtime capsule objects + link
// libs (`_clang_link_test_cmd_with_deps`), which are not part of the
// test's own dep closure. Those are folded in via `runtime_identity`
// (`runtime_link_inputs_identity` — the runtime source + link-lib
// content hashed by the caller), so a runtime edit busts the key
// directly by content. This replaced the former reliance on a
// per-commit `compiler_identity` busting every entry on every commit
// (#1233): `compiler_identity` is now the bare capsule version plus the
// emitting compiler binary's content hash (`test_bin_compiler_identity`).
// Identical compiler bytes therefore hit across commits while different
// compiler binaries always miss. `--no-test-cache` on the `make check` /
// full-suite gate remains the final cold-build backstop.
//
//   key = sha256(
//       sha256(test_source_bytes)
//       || sha256(sorted(dep hashes))   // dep_ll files + native_texts
//       || compiler_identity            // capsule version + binary SHA-256
//       || runtime_identity             // runtime_link_inputs_identity(...)
//       || canonical(clang_flags)
//       || test_bin_cache_schema_version()
//       || "tgt1:" || target_triple
//   )
//
// Hashes are sorted before folding so the key is invariant under dep
// enumeration order. `unique_suffix` disambiguates the `_sha256_of_string`
// temp path across concurrent `sfn test` invocations on different
// files — pass the (sanitized) test source path; reused sequentially
// within one process, never across two processes hashing the same
// file simultaneously (the module cache carries the identical
// single-process precondition).
//
// SFN-676: `target_triple` folds in the build TARGET, mirroring
// `cache_key_for`'s SFN-677 fix for the module `.ll` cache — without it
// a cross-target `sfn test` run could restore a linked binary built for
// a different OS/triple. It goes LAST with nothing appended after it
// under its own `tgt1:` marker (independent of `cache_key_for`'s
// `tgt3:`, which has been bumped twice for defects specific to that
// key): the value may originate from an unvalidated
// `SAILFIN_TARGET_TRIPLE`/`SAILFIN_TARGET_OS` env string and so may
// contain any byte, including the separators used here. Appending
// anything derived from it afterward would let a crafted value absorb
// its own successor and alias a different tuple — the exact defect
// `cache_key_for`'s docstring records for its own `target_triple` fold.
fn test_bin_cache_key(test_source_path: string, dep_ll_paths: string[], native_texts: string[], compiler_identity: string, runtime_identity: string, flags_canonical: string, unique_suffix: string, target_triple: string) -> CacheKey ![io] {
    let s_hash = _sha256_of_file(test_source_path);

    let mut dep_hashes: string[] = [];
    let mut i: int = 0;
    loop {
        if i >= dep_ll_paths.length { break; }
        dep_hashes.push(_sha256_of_file(dep_ll_paths[i]));
        i += 1;
    }
    let mut j: int = 0;
    loop {
        if j >= native_texts.length { break; }
        dep_hashes.push(_sha256_of_string(native_texts[j], unique_suffix + "_nt"));
        j += 1;
    }

    let sorted_dep_hashes = _sort_strings(dep_hashes);
    let mut deps_concat: string = "";
    let mut k: int = 0;
    loop {
        if k >= sorted_dep_hashes.length { break; }
        if k > 0 { deps_concat = deps_concat + "|"; }
        deps_concat = deps_concat + sorted_dep_hashes[k];
        k += 1;
    }
    let deps_hash = _sha256_of_string(deps_concat, unique_suffix + "_deps");

    let final_input = s_hash + "\n" + deps_hash + "\n" + compiler_identity
        + "\n"
        + runtime_identity
        + "\n"
        + flags_canonical
        + "\n"
        + test_bin_cache_schema_version()
        + "\ntgt1:"
        + target_triple;
    let final_digest = _sha256_of_string(final_input, unique_suffix + "_final");
    return CacheKey { digest: final_digest };
}

// Content identity for the runtime link inputs a test binary links
// against (#1233). The clang link pulls in the assembled runtime capsule
// objects + link libs, which are NOT part of the test's own dep closure;
// folding their *source* content into the test-bin key is what lets that
// cache stay correct across a runtime edit WITHOUT relying on a
// per-commit compiler stamp (which would bust every entry on every
// commit). Callers enumerate the runtime inputs once per `sfn test`
// invocation (the set is invocation-stable) and fold the result into
// `test_bin_cache_key` as `runtime_identity`.
//
// `source_paths` are the runtime input sources (`.c` / `.ll` / `.sfn`
// + prelude entry + the `.h` headers under the include roots) hashed off
// disk; `value_inputs` are non-file inputs folded by value — linker flag
// strings (`-lm`, `-lpthread`, capsule-declared libs) and the include-dir
// paths (`-I<dir>`), so an include-root or link-flag change busts the key.
// All components are sorted before folding so the identity is invariant
// under enumeration order. Each source folds in as `<path>@<sha256>`: the
// path keeps two distinct inputs from aliasing (notably when a file is
// unreadable and `_sha256_of_file` returns an empty digest) and makes
// a rename observable. Hashing SOURCES (not built objects) is both
// available on the single-file leaf path — where objects may not exist
// yet — and strictly upstream: a source edit busts even before a rebuild.
fn runtime_link_inputs_identity(source_paths: string[], value_inputs: string[], unique_suffix: string) -> string ![io] {
    let mut parts: string[] = [];
    let mut i: int = 0;
    loop {
        if i >= source_paths.length { break; }
        parts.push("src:" + source_paths[i] + "@" + _sha256_of_file(source_paths[i]));
        i += 1;
    }
    let mut j: int = 0;
    loop {
        if j >= value_inputs.length { break; }
        parts.push("val:" + value_inputs[j]);
        j += 1;
    }
    let sorted = _sort_strings(parts);
    let mut concat: string = "";
    let mut k: int = 0;
    loop {
        if k >= sorted.length { break; }
        if k > 0 { concat = concat + "|"; }
        concat = concat + sorted[k];
        k += 1;
    }
    return _sha256_of_string(concat, unique_suffix + "_rt");
}

